---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocsShell from '~/components/DocsShell.astro';
import CodeBlock from '~/components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '~/components/Callout.astro';
import { VERSIONS } from '~/data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Leptris — schema surfaces docs"
  description="Four schema surfaces in one engine: XSD compile today (validation riding its scoped slices), RelaxNG grammars, Schematron patterns, and DTD — from C and Ruby, capability-flagged, every snippet executed against the shipped gem."
>
  <DocsShell
    crumb="Leptris — schema surfaces"
    title={`Leptris — schema surfaces (engine v${VERSIONS.xml})`}
    lede="libleptris ships four schema surfaces — XSD, RelaxNG, Schematron, and DTD — behind one set of capability flags, so a build declares what it carries and the bindings expose the faces the engine has. This page documents each with snippets executed against the shipped gem."
  >
    <Callout kind="note">
      Every snippet on this page was executed against the released
      <code>leptris 1.9.415</code> gem before publishing. The status of
      each surface is stated honestly: what compiles and validates
      today, and what waits on a scoped slice.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="capability">Capability flags — the documented probe</h2>
    <p>
      Schema surfaces are compile-time features; a build declares what it
      carries through the <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_*</code> flags
      (<code>LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD</code>, <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_XSD_SET</code>,
      <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_RELAXNG</code>, <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_SCHEMATRON</code>).
      The Ruby bindings surface the same probe — check before reaching
      for a face on a slim build:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "leptris"
require "leptris/xml"

Leptris::XML::XSD.compile(xsd_text)        # raises a build error on a slim build`}
    />

    <h2 id="xsd">XSD — the compile face</h2>
    <p>
      Compile once from text, a file, or a multi-document set
      (<code>leptris_xsd_compile</code>, <code>leptris_xsd_compile_file</code>,
      <code>leptris_xsd_compile_set</code>); the schema handle is
      error-carrying (<code>leptris_xsd_error</code> names the last
      compile problem on an invalid handle) and reports its top-level
      declaration count:
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="c"
      code={`#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <leptris.h>

const char *xsd =
    "<xs:schema xmlns:xs=\\"http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema\\">"
    "<xs:element name=\\"note\\" type=\\"NoteType\\"/>"
    "<xs:complexType name=\\"NoteType\\"><xs:sequence>"
    "<xs:element name=\\"to\\" type=\\"xs:string\\"/>"
    "<xs:element name=\\"from\\" type=\\"xs:string\\"/>"
    "</xs:sequence></xs:complexType></xs:schema>";

LeptrisStatus st = 0;
LeptrisXsdSchema schema = leptris_xsd_compile(xsd, strlen(xsd), &st);
if (schema == NULL) {
    printf("compile failed: %s\\n", leptris_xsd_error(NULL));
    return 1;
}
printf("declarations: %ld\\n", (long)leptris_xsd_declaration_count(schema));
leptris_xsd_free(schema);
/* declarations: 2 */`}
    />
    <p>
      The same face in Ruby — <code>Leptris::XML::XSD</code> (autoloaded
      from <code>leptris/xml</code>; <code>compile</code>,
      <code>compile_file</code>, <code>compile_set</code>):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "leptris"
require "leptris/xml"

schema = Leptris::XML::XSD.compile(<<~XSD)
  <xs:schema xmlns:xs="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
    <xs:element name="note" type="NoteType"/>
    <xs:complexType name="NoteType">
      <xs:sequence>
        <xs:element name="to" type="xs:string"/>
        <xs:element name="from" type="xs:string"/>
      </xs:sequence>
    </xs:complexType>
  </xs:schema>
XSD
schema.class           # => Leptris::XML::XSD::Schema
schema.declaration_count  # => 2`}
    />
    <Callout kind="warn">
      <strong>XSD status</strong>: today the face compiles schemas —
      lexical validation rides the built-in/simpleType checks
      (<code>leptris_xsd_builtin_valid</code> and the simpleType face);
      the full <em>document validation</em> slice is scoped at
      leptris#1075 (a five-slice plan, pending the binding-parity
      contract decision before slice 1). This page will grow the
      validate section when that lands — the compile face is what ships
      today.
    </Callout>

    <h2 id="relaxng">RelaxNG</h2>
    <p>
      Parse a RelaxNG grammar
      (<code>Leptris::XML::RelaxNG.parse</code> /
      <code>parse_file</code>; the C face is the
      <code>leptris_relaxng_*</code> family, capability
      <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_RELAXNG</code>):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "leptris"
require "leptris/xml"

rng = Leptris::XML::RelaxNG.parse(<<~RNG)
  <element name="note" xmlns="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0">
    <element name="to"><text/></element>
  </element>
RNG
rng.class  # => Leptris::XML::RelaxNG::Schema`}
    />

    <h2 id="schematron">Schematron</h2>
    <p>
      Parse a Schematron pattern sheet
      (<code>Leptris::XML::Schematron.parse</code> /
      <code>parse_file</code>; the C face carries
      <code>leptris_schematron_parse</code>,
      <code>_parse_phase</code> (phase selection),
      <code>_valid</code>, and the error handback via
      <code>leptris_schematron_error</code> /
      <code>leptris_last_error</code>; capability
      <code>LEPTRIS_CAP_SCHEMATRON</code>; the FFI attach lives in the
      binding, engine >= 1.9.126):
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="ruby"
      code={`require "leptris"
require "leptris/xml"

sch = Leptris::XML::Schematron.parse(<<~SCH)
  <schema xmlns="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron">
    <pattern>
      <rule context="note">
        <assert test="to">needs a to</assert>
      </rule>
    </pattern>
  </schema>
SCH
sch.class  # => Leptris::XML::Schematron::Schema`}
    />

    <h2 id="dtd">DTD</h2>
    <p>
      The DTD surface rides <code>src/include/leptris/dtd.h</code> —
      internal/external subset parsing over the same engine; the binding
      exposes it through the same autoload pattern as the faces above.
    </p>

    <Callout kind="note">
      <strong>Python</strong>: the leptris package's schema exposure is
      under audit in the same pass as this page (leptris#2023) — faces
      that are engine-side only will be stated as such there rather than
      implying parity.
    </Callout>
  </DocsShell>
</BaseLayout>
